        <h1>LeetCode 462: Minimum Moves to Equal Array Elements II (C++)</h1>
        <div class="problem-description">
            <p><strong>Problem:</strong>
                Given an integer array nums of size n, return the minimum number of moves required to make
                all array elements equal. In one move, you can increment or decrement an element of the
                array by 1. Test cases are designed so that the answer will fit in a 32-bit integer.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Example:</strong>
                Input: nums = [1,2,3]<br>
                Output: 2<br>
                Explanation: Only two moves are needed (remember each move increments or decrements one
                element): [1,2,3] =&gt; [2,2,3] =&gt; [2,2,2].
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="code-container">
            <button class="copy-button" onclick="copyCode(this)">Copy Code</button>
            <pre><code class="language-cpp">class Solution {
public:
    int minMoves2(vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; nums) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        int medianValue = nums[nums.size() / 2], result = 0;
        for (int value : nums) result += abs(medianValue - value);
        return result;
    }
};</code></pre>
